Sarcasm Detection Model 🎭

A Machine Learning project that detects sarcasm in text using a Soft-Voting Ensemble approach. unlike modern heavy-weight Transformer models, this project utilizes efficient feature engineering and traditional ML algorithms to achieve ~82% accuracy by leveraging lexical cues and punctuation patterns.

This model operates under the constraint of not using Transformers (BERT, GPT, etc.), we focused on:

Custom Preprocessing: preserving punctuation (e.g., !!!, ...) as distinct features rather than stripping them.

TF-IDF Vectorization: Using Bigrams (1,2) to capture sarcastic phrases like "Yeah right".

Ensemble Learning: Combining Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting.


Installation & Setup

1. Clone the Repository
git clone [https://github.com/beginnerxo/Sarcasm.git](https://github.com/beginnerxo/Sarcasm.git)
cd Sarcasm

2. Install Dependencies in a venv.

pip install -r requirements.txt




🛠️ Usage

A. Training the Model
To retrain the ensemble model from scratch using the dataset in data/:

python train_model.py

This will preprocess the data, train the ensemble, and save the serialized pipeline to models/sarcasm_model.pk1.

B. Running Predictions
To generate predictions on a new CSV file:

python predict_sarcasm.py --input "data/test_data.csv" --output "predictions.csv"

🧠 Model Architecture
The core model is a VotingClassifier (Soft Voting) consisting of three estimators:

Logistic Regression
The Baseline
Captures linear relationships and strong lexical cues (e.g., "obviously").

Random Forest
The Pattern Matcher
Captures non-linear dependencies and punctuation patterns (100 estimators).

Gradient Boosting
The Specialist
Corrects errors from previous trees; handles edge cases well.

Preprocessing Pipeline:
Input: "Great weather!!!"
Custom Tokenization: "great ! ! !" (Punctuation is isolated, not removed).
Vectorization: TF-IDF with (1,2) n-grams, min_df=2.
📊 Performance
The model was evaluated on a held-out test set of 966 samples:
Accuracy: 82.09%
F1-Score: 80.80%
Precision (Non-Sarcastic): 0.85
Precision (Sarcastic): 0.79

Note: The model performs exceptionally well on structural sarcasm (heavy punctuation usage) but naturally faces challenges with purely contextual sarcasm due to the lack of deep semantic embeddings.

📂 Project Structure

├── data/                  # Training and test datasets
├── models/                # Saved serialized models (.pk1)
├── src/
│   └── utils.py           # Custom preprocessing logic
├── sarcasm_app.py         # Streamlit frontend
├── train_model.py         # Training script
├── predict_sarcasm.py     # Inference script
├── requirements.txt       # Project dependencies
└── README.md              # Project documentation


